  1242	
  1243	A record is written only when its level is at or above the threshold. The levels
  1244	are ordered `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`, and the threshold is one of those
  1245	four or `off`. The **default is `off`**, so a call added to a library writes
  1246	nothing and cannot change what the program using it prints; a program has to ask
  1247	for diagnostics before it gets any. The threshold is set with
  1248	`logging.set_level` and read back with `logging.level`. `logging.reset` restores
  1249	the defaults.
  1250	
  1251	The destination is `stderr` by default, which keeps diagnostics off the
  1252	program's output stream, and `logging.set_destination` takes either the exact
  1253	text `stderr`, the exact text `stdout`, or any other text as a file path. A file
  1254	destination is appended to, one record per write, through the same filesystem as
  1255	`files.write`, so a record written by one engine is readable by the other. A
  1256	destination that cannot be written — a missing directory, a directory instead of
  1257	a file — is a catchable `IoError` naming `logging`, not a process abort. On Unix
  1258	the same holds for a genuinely closed or broken `stdout`/`stderr` descriptor; the
  1259	suite pins that with a read-only descriptor and a pipe whose reader is gone. Other
  1260	targets write through the standard handle, which reports a closed or broken stream
  1261	as the same `IoError`; the suite cannot close a child's descriptor portably, so
  1262	that path is exercised on Unix only.
  1263	
  1264	A record has the shape `[timestamp] [LEVEL] message`, or `[LEVEL] message` when
  1265	timestamps are off. Timestamps are **on by default**, because a log a reader
  1266	cannot place in time is less useful; they make a record nondeterministic, so
  1267	`logging.set_timestamps(no)` is the deterministic mode and is what a golden
  1268	output or a corpus comparison should use. The timestamp is UTC, to the
  1269	millisecond, in the form `2026-11-03T12:34:56.789Z`. A host with no clock — the
  1270	WebAssembly playground before `rb_set_clock` — writes the record without the
  1271	timestamp field rather than failing the run or aborting; the same situation
  1272	makes `time.now()` a catchable error, because a logging call still has a record
  1273	to write. The message is written exactly as given, including an empty message
  1274	and the newlines of a multi-line one; the record itself ends with one newline.
  1275	
  1276	`logging.debug`, `logging.info`, `logging.warn` and `logging.error` each take one
  1277	message. The four are functions of the module, so their bare spellings are not a
  1278	second surface. The tree-walking VM and the bytecode VM share one implementation
  1279	of the threshold, destination and formatting, so the same program writes the
  1280	same records under both.
  1281	
